# ── Client configuration ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything here is inlined into the JavaScript bundle by Metro and ships to
# the device. Treat this file as public. Copy it to `.env` and fill in.

# Supabase. The anon key is *designed* to be public: it is not an authorisation,
# and every request it makes is still evaluated against Row Level Security using
# the caller's own JWT. A stolen anon key grants exactly what an anonymous
# visitor already has.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-key


# ── What must NEVER go in this file ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# OPENAI_API_KEY
#   An OpenAI key is an authorisation to spend, with no per-user scoping. In a
#   client bundle it is extractable with `strings` (no jailbreak required) and
#   the bill is yours. It belongs to the chat Edge Function:
#
#       supabase secrets set OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
#       supabase secrets set OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini      # optional
#
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
#   Bypasses Row Level Security entirely: one leaked copy and every patient's
#   prep record is readable by anyone. Supabase injects it into Edge Functions
#   automatically; you never need to set it yourself, and it must never be
#   prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_.
#
# If either ever appears in a commit, rotate it. Deleting the commit is not
# enough: assume anything pushed has been scraped.
